    <h1>Structure is a decision,<br />not an accident.</h1>

    <p class="hero__lead">
      OTAKU is a philosophy for object-oriented software. Five constraints decide
      what code is allowed to exist; three shapes decide how repositories are
      organized. Nothing here belongs to a language — only to the paradigm.
    </p>

    <ul class="actions">
      <li><a class="primary" href="/manifesto">Read the manifesto</a></li>
      <li><a href="/guide">Open the guide</a></li>
      <li><a href="/shapes">The three shapes</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Why this exists</h2>

  <p>
    Frameworks are excellent until you need to replace one piece — and then demand
    that you replace all of them. The usual escape is a rewrite, which replaces one
    set of accidental constraints with another. OTAKU is the opposite bet:
    architecture made of parts small enough to understand in one sitting, and
    replaceable without permission.
  </p>

  <p>
    The name is an acronym, and also a temperament. It describes a technical
    obsession with mastery, discipline and structural purity — the willingness to
    delete a clever abstraction because it cannot be explained in a sentence.
  </p>

  <h2>How to read this book</h2>

  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href="/manifesto">
      <span class="card__num">01</span>
