    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>What it refuses</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Frameworks leaking into the domain layer.</li>
    <li>Repositories that cannot be classified as atom, assembly or application.</li>
    <li>Abstractions created before the second real use case exists.</li>
    <li>Code that needs a comment to explain its indentation.</li>
    <li>Interfaces published without a contract.</li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    Write code that never needs “fixing” because it was built under absolute
    constraint.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Scope</h2>

  <p>
    OTAKU was born inside <a href="https://phprise.org">PHPRise</a>, but PHP is only
    where it started. The rules are about indentation, collections, dependency
    direction, contracts and naming — concepts every object-oriented language
    expresses. The guide is written against <strong>PHP, C#, Java and C++</strong>,
    and deliberately avoids idioms that only one of them can express.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your language has classes, interfaces, exceptions and a package manager, the
    philosophy applies. If it does not, this is not the right tool — and that is a
    legitimate answer.
  </p>
